<div id="clickable0" class="clickable">
  <a>
    &#8505
  </a>
</div>

<div id="content0" class="content">
    The Computational Relative Value Matrix aims to uncover the opportunities in the relative value movements of european government bond markets. It takes a hierarchical approach, moving from major principal components of the market, through multi-bond curve disjoints within countries, or between countries, and further right down to individual bond relative value. Extensive use is made of visualization charts, as the model is aimed at <i> decision support </i>, and it is a core principle of the model that portfolio managers should be able to see what is happening in the RV markets, rather than being prescribed ideas in an opaque form. For more information on how to use CRVM, please contact the team on +442086054396.
</div>
<div id = "main" style="clear: both;">
    <hr>| <a href="http://www.itcmarkets.com/wp-content/uploads/2014/10/Understanding-CRVM-Real-Money.pdf">
    Understanding the ITC CRVM model output 
</a> | <a href="#direction">Directional</a> | <a href="#trisyield">Sector Returns</a> | <a href="#heatmaps">RV Heatmap</a> | <a href="#cdcurves">Country RV curves</a> | <a href="#returns">Returns Analysis</a> |
<hr><h2> Market Direction</h2>

<div id="clickable1" class="clickable">
  <a>
    &#8505
  </a>
</div>

<div id="content1" class="content">
    Principal Components Analysis is a technique for reducing the analytical complexity of high dimensional data sets. It operates on the correlation matrix of a data set, and extracts the driving factors in descending order of importance. Thus portfolio managers are able to focus on the 3-4 factors which typically account for over 95% of the risk associated with a market. In the case below, PCA is applies to the matrix of all country benchmark yields (2y, 3y, 5y, 7y, 10y, 20y and 30y) together. 
    PC1 represents market level, PC2 represents the peripheral spreads, PC3 represents the market slope, and PC4 represents the market curvature. Note that in single country PCA, PC2 usually represents slope. With CRVM however, spread risk is more important than slope risk, and thus this dominates PC2. Notably also, PC4 for spain and Italy does not represent curvature, but reflects the outperformance of Italy versus Spain when the curvature component in other countries increases. This may be due to the fact that when policy is aggressive enough to see Italian yields lower (after accounting for PCs 1-3), the market tends to become nervous about the sustainability of such policy, to the (marginal) detriment of credibility. 
    The loadings on the principal components explain how much each maturity in each country contributes to the principal component in question. They help significantly in interpretation of the components. 
    The major goal of PCA is parsimony of analysis angles, that is, allowing risk takers to focus on the small number of variables that drive markets. Indeed, later on in the CRVM, analyses of relative curve shape or bond cheap/dear will be performed <i> after </i> extracting the effects of principal components. 
</div>


