    heatarrange <- grid.arrange(heatmaps$heatmain, heatmaps$heatevol, ncol = 2)
    ggstamp(cettime())
    end.rcode-->

<hr>| <a href="http://www.itcmarkets.com/wp-content/uploads/2013/02/Understanding-CRVM2.pdf">
    Understanding the ITC CRVM model output 
</a> | <a href="#direction">Directional</a> | <a href="#trisyield">Sector Returns</a> | <a href="#heatmaps">RV Heatmap</a> | <a href="#cdcurves">Country RV curves</a> | <a href="#returns">Returns Analysis</a> |
<hr><h2> Country RV Magnifier</h2>


<div id="clickable4" class="clickable">
  <a>
    &#8505
  </a>
</div>

<div id="content4" class="content">
    In the RV Magnifier charts, we combine the curve sector cheap dear, and individual bond cheap dear, for each country. This should be the main chart used by portfolio managers before they invest in any bond or sector of any country. At a glance, the manager may see whether a sector of the curve is cheap (blue), neutral (yellow), or dear (red). The same colour coding applies to individual bonds. The boxplots behind the bonds show where each bond has traded in the past relative to the curve. The shaded area in the background of the graphic, shows the 1 (dotted white) and 2 (solid white) standard deviation thresholds for curve cheap dear. Please keep in mind that these analyses are performed <i> after </i> extraction of the principal components of the market. In other words, everything in these charts is <i> independent </i> of market level, spread, slope, and curvature. They exist to allow the portfolio manager to extract micro-alpha, independently of positioning in the other market principal components. 
</div>

